WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:13.440 align:start position:0%
 
let us pray  Oh God may the words of my mouth and the  meditations of all of our hearts be  acceptable in your sight for you are our  rock and our Redeemer amen good morning  it's so good to be here with you this  morning and I especially thank our  pastor Ian for this kind invitation and  our chaplain Sharon for her leadership  and religious life in this fine  University it is such a privilege to  have the opportunity to share some  thoughts on Scripture and on the Trinity  and on réunion a Sunday here as I travel  with my family my daughter JJ and my  husband Dan for my 20th reunion hey gene  hey Erica great to see everybody here  feels like 20 years ago all over again  I'm having flashbacks it's not always  good but it's mostly and I take as my  text this morning the twelfth verse of  the sixteenth chapter of the Gospel  according to John I take as my text this  verse I still have many things to say to  you but you cannot bear them now Jesus  says to his disciples I have many things  to say to you but you cannot bear them  now in the portion of the passage that  follows this verse jesus foreshadows  that the Holy Spirit will come and will  shape and prepare the disciples to  continue in their ministries of loving  teaching and healing to anyone the  concept of the holy spirit the third  person of the Trinity who acts as a  metaphysical helper to Jesus's followers  is pretty abstract even worse Jesus is  foreshadowing this abstract concept to  the disciples before they  only know that Jesus is not going to be  with them for much longer they don't  know yet that Jesus will be arrested  while they fear for their own safety and  that after the devastating loss of  Jesus's crucifixion he will be raised  from the dead and will ascend into  heaven in other words Jesus is  foreshadowing an abstract metaphysical  concept to a group of people that hasn't  been through what it would need to have  gone through in order to have the  vaguest notion of what he was talking  about no simple task knowing that the  disciple disciples have no frame of  reference for what Jesus is hinting at  Jesus speaks obliquely and vaguely and  tells them that he's limiting his words  based on their lack of readiness he says  I still have many things to say to you  but you cannot bear them now maybe Jesus  would like very much to talk with his  friends about what's going to happen and  maybe they would very much like to know  when Jesus tells them that they aren't  ready I'm sure he's right but i can't  imagine his disciples agreeing with him  i don't know what your experience has  been in the past when it comes to  telling people they're not ready for  something in my experience those  conversations haven't gone particularly  well ever told a young adult that you  don't think he's ready to get married  right after he's come to you with  excitement and joy ever told an employee  that you don't think she's ready for the  promotion for which she's been striving  ever told anybody that you don't think  they're ready to hear something that  they think they want to know  conversations like these are hard on  everyone they're hard on the one doing  the saying and that the hearer doesn't  know what he or she doesn't know and the  Sayer can't really explain that those  conversations are hard on the hearer in  that the one who hears might actually  feel quite ready to hear and believe  simply that he's not being understood  even worse the one hearing she isn't  ready might on some deep level actually  know that the one who's saying so is  right and that hurts even more I had an  experience just a couple of days ago  where somebody told me I was not ready  for something me I was not ready for  something really seriously yeah Sarah  you're not ready you're not ready do I  sound bitter  do I sound the little little consumed  now not not no Viet not in the least not  unless there's no big deal there's no  big deal at all well I wanted to work on  a particular writing project related to  my research and it's kind of a long  story but the upshot was this I was  given the feedback that my research idea  didn't seem ready for prime time I  needed a lot more work than I thought it  did and would have to be delayed until  both I and my idea were more ready again  this was no big deal really I didn't  want to have a productive summer anyway  with this recent exchange I had a chance  to ride the wave of what it feels like  to be told I'm not ready for something I  felt myself relating to the disciples in  our passage today imagining what it  would have been like to hear Jesus say  that they no matter how much they loved  him and cherished him and strove to  follow him they weren't ready to hear  his words the feelings that came over me  were confusion hurt rejection hearing we  aren't ready doesn't feel very good it  doesn't feel very good at all father  Michael Himes is a Jesuit theologian at  Boston College who created a video  resource a few years ago for college  students on discerning vocation or  figuring out their sense of calling in  this resource father Himes names what he  calls three key questions and he says  that we must ask ourselves the questions  in order to know where God is leading us  in our lives the questions are first  what gives me joy  second what am I good at and third does  anybody need me to do it father Himes  takes great pains with the second of  those three key questions what am I good  at he makes clear that people can figure  out on their own what gives them joy and  they can figure out on their own where  opportunities to serve might exist but  to figure out what we're good at  actually requires other people other  people actually have to tell us whether  we are good singers or joke tellers or  biochemists or ministers in the same way  we often have to rely on other people to  tell us whether or not we are ready for  something we kind of always think we are  but even when I consider younger  versions of myself I know that this is  simply not true there were lots of  things that younger versions of Sarah  thought she was ready for and lots of  cases where a younger Sarah was wrong I  needed others to help me see that in my  post Yale days I got really interested  in adult learning theory among the five  or six widely agreed upon nearly  universally accepted principles of adult  learning is the concept that adults  can't learn something they're not ready  to learn readiness is one of the  required precursors of adult education  the logical question for adult learners  is therefore what does it take  to become ready to learn adult learning  theorists most notably Malcolm Knowles  argue that adults learn what they need  to learn in order to adjust to life  situations in other words we become  ready to learn because we have to an  adult who loves somebody with a  disability can quickly become an expert  in treating or living with that  disability Kate Rhea and Morgan O'Neill  who survived the tornado in munson  Massachusetts last May created resources  on community organizing around disasters  that volunteers in Moore Oklahoma are  using right now they learned what they  knew about disaster relief through an  experience for which they did not ask  necessity breeds readiness we can see  echoes of this form of readiness the  readiness bread of necessity and so many  other parts of life I teach ministry  students about faith development in my  classes at Andover Newton theological  school one thing we know about faith  development is that our face evolved as  we are ready for them to evolve but they  become ready to evolve when they have to  consider this example a young man  immature in his face feels threatened by  the beliefs of another he worries that  the other person's beliefs will infect  him with doubt he encounters someone of  another faith through a life situation  he can't control when he is assigned a  roommate raised in another religious  tradition suddenly there's no more room  for him to avoid the other person's  beliefs quite literally  no room so his faith has to evolve so he  doesn't feel threatened all the time so  he becomes more mature in his beliefs in  order to flourish in a new life  circumstance similarly anyone who's  worked with the addicted knows that an  addict can only find help in treatment  when she's ready that readiness often  shows up in an addicted person who's  fallen in love or had a child because  the addict needs to find healing in  order to hold on to something more  important suddenly she's ready to learn  what recovery would take I'm here  celebrating my 20th college reunion as I  make my way through my visit this  weekend I am painfully aware of how much  I didn't learn here because I wasn't  ready to learn it the years I spent at  Yale were among the four happiest of my  life and there's no institution in my  personal history about which I have fond  or feelings than Yale but I could have  been here a hundred years and still felt  like I missed a lot of learning  opportunities and a lot of other things  too yet when I consider that which I did  learn I can see in retrospect I learned  what I had to I learned within an hour  of living on Elm how to cross an urban  street with the light I learned how to  live away from my parents to make  conversation with a nun housed neighbor  and identify my own passions when faced  with an overwhelming array of options  jesus knew the disciples weren't ready  to understand the concept of the Holy  Spirit at the moment he was trying to  tell  them about it they had to go through  hell to get to a point where they could  possibly grasp the notion of a good  metaphysical friend that would be God's  presence among them during what would  become Jesus's very long absence in a  way this might have been a blissfully  ignorant moment for the disciples we  have to remember unreadiness isn't all  bad necessity breeds readiness but  necessity isn't always very much fun it  can kind of stink sometimes necessity  the 90s metal band poison might sing  sometimes I wish I didn't know now the  things I didn't know then the disciples  weren't ready to understand the idea of  the Holy Spirit because Jesus was still  with them they hadn't yet experienced  the horror of his humiliating death nor  had they witnessed the glory of his  triumphant resurrection but in their  unreadiness and this is the important  part they didn't do anything wrong  they're only human just like us this is  the good news there's nothing wrong with  not being ready for something there was  nothing wrong with my Yale self who  didn't know she should have tried crew  she should have taken a course with  Peter salle ave I mean seriously how did  I not take a course with Peter Salome  should have spent even more time with my  friends although I did spend a lot  should have fed even more time with my  friends when they lived no more than a  city block away  many people advised me to do all of  those things when I was a student here  but I wasn't ready to hear them oh well  what are you going to do the feeling we  have of sadness or in competence when  we're told that we aren't ready is the  voice of shame and the voice of regret  in us it is not the voice of reason it  is not the voice of kindness and it is  definitely not the voice of God God at  work in us helps us to say through the  Holy Spirit you know what maybe I'm not  ready God isn't finished with me yet as  a Christian I believe that we know now  only in part and only God knows fully I  believe that Jesus was God made  incarnate on the earth and we see in  Jesus glimmers of what God is and what  God knows about us we strive over the  course of a whole lifetime to follow  Jesus and to be more like him and as we  do that we become more and more ready to  learn who God is and what God is calling  us to become in our passage today we see  that jesus knew his disciples he knew  what they were and weren't ready for in  the same way God knows us and reveals  God's plan for us on God's time not our  time as we try to place our trust in God  we face moments when we're told we  aren't ready and we face them with  humility and we face them with hope  we welcome the life experiences that  will help us to be ready to learn and we  trust that they'll bring us closer to  the God who knows us who knows  everything and is our ultimate  destination thanks be to God amen

